1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A synthetic SQL request has reached a route
with current evidence that it is free.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The route returns HTTP four twenty-nine. That means temporary
capacity pressure, not permanent model invalidation.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:25,000
ZeroSpend blocks the paid branch, tries another verified-free
candidate, and completes the request without silent spend.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:34,000
ZeroSpend is a local, OpenAI-compatible routing layer for
workloads that must remain on currently verified-free routes.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Its central rule is simple: unknown cost is not free.
When evidence is ambiguous, the route is ineligible.

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:53,000
We will begin from a clean temporary home, so the recording contains
no personal paths, shell history, or existing configuration.

7
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:03,000
ZeroSpend currently requires macOS, Node.js twenty-two
or later, npm, and Git for this source installation.

8
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Clone the repository, enter the project directory, and point
HOME and ZEROSPEND_HOME at an isolated temporary location.

9
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Run the installer without sudo. It creates a user-owned
launcher and keeps the router and console bound to loopback.

10
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:33,000
No provider credential is entered here. The clean installation remains
recoverable even before interactive provider setup is complete.

11
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:43,000
The components command reports the runtime, the local router,
the console, supported clients, and optional execution backends.

12
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Hermes is the recommended client, but it is not required.
Any compatible OpenAI client can call the same endpoint.

13
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:05,000
The router classifies each request deterministically, maps
it to a task alias, and orders only eligible candidates.

14
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Provider adapters execute requests, while the console reads operational
metadata without storing prompts, completions, SQL, or tool payloads.

15
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Provider setup is shared by the terminal and local console. Bearer
credentials are entered with hidden input and stored locally.

16
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:38,000
OpenRouter is a direct OpenAI-compatible provider. Its free evidence
requires explicit zero pricing and an eligible free model identifier.

17
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:49,000
NVIDIA NIM is also direct. A route needs current official
Free Endpoint evidence before ZeroSpend can admit it.

18
00:02:49,000 --> 00:03:00,000
OpenCode Free follows a different path: ZeroSpend calls
local 9Router, which then executes the OpenCode Free request.

19
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:13,000
OpenCode Free needs no provider credential, but backend reachability,
current catalog evidence, local mapping, and a bounded probe still matter.

20
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:26,000
OpenCode Zen is never substituted. All provider names, model
names, counts, and availability states shown here are demo data.

21
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:36,000
A model appearing in a provider catalog is only the first gate.
Listing alone says nothing definitive about production eligibility.

22
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:46,000
Next, ZeroSpend checks provider-specific cost evidence. Explicit
zero cost may proceed; unknown or ambiguous cost is rejected.

23
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:57,000
The candidate must also support the request requirements, such
as tools, structured output, context size, or task category.

24
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:08,000
A small bounded probe then checks current execution availability
without creating a retry storm or consuming excessive free quota.

25
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Only after those gates does ZeroSpend create a timestamped
production-eligibility decision for that specific route.

26
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:30,000
The Models and Providers views expose the evidence source, probe
result, current capacity state, and last verification time.

27
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Before sending traffic, run the doctor command and inspect the strict-free
counters. Paid routes and unverified routes should both be zero.

28
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:50,000
The router exposes one loopback OpenAI-compatible endpoint,
while the console uses a separate loopback management boundary.

29
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Unknown cost never becomes an emergency fallback. A capacity
failure cannot silently widen the policy to a paid route.

30
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:10,000
The Safety view also confirms that content is excluded from
telemetry and local services are not exposed by default.

31
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Now send an explicitly synthetic streaming request to the
local chat-completions endpoint. The local token stays hidden.

32
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:30,000
The request and generated answer are intentionally omitted from
the recording. We only inspect routing headers and metadata.

33
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:40,000
The classifier recognizes the SQL task and selects the
free-sql alias without making a second language-model call.

34
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Candidate A is currently eligible because its cost evidence,
capabilities, and bounded availability result all passed.

35
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:00,000
For this synthetic scenario, candidate A returns
HTTP four twenty-nine after the request begins.

36
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:10,000
ZeroSpend records temporary capacity pressure. It does not erase
the model evidence or mark the identifier permanently unsupported.

37
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:20,000
The policy evaluates the next branch. A paid or unverified
route is not eligible, so that branch is explicitly blocked.

38
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Candidate B has independent current free evidence. It receives
the request and returns a synthetic HTTP two hundred response.

39
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
The console row updates with fallback depth one.

40
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Live Routing shows the selected alias, provider and model,
status, latency, token counts, attempts, and fallback depth.

41
00:06:48,000 --> 00:07:01,000
The Routing view explains candidate order. Task score, recent
reliability, and bounded benchmark evidence inform the ranking.

42
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:14,000
Models separates catalog membership, cost evidence, capability
support, availability, and final production eligibility.

43
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Providers separates configuration from catalog reachability and current
capacity. A configured provider can still have zero eligible routes.

44
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Benchmarks are small synthetic routing signals, not a universal model
leaderboard. Failed evaluation does not overwrite a known-good ranking.

45
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Usage records aggregate prompt and completion token counts by
route, but never stores the underlying prompt or generated text.

46
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Performance combines latency, time to first token, success history,
errors, and recent rate-limit state without continuous background testing.

47
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Safety keeps the invariant visible: zero paid production routes,
zero unverified production routes, and loopback-only defaults.

48
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Overview brings the same evidence together so an operator
can audit why a route was selected and how fallback behaved.

49
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Here is the complete path again. A compatible client
sends one request to ZeroSpend on the local endpoint.

50
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:56,000
ZeroSpend owns classification, evidence policy, ordering, and metadata.
Execution is direct, or passes through 9Router for OpenCode Free.

51
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:10,000
For Hermes, configure a custom OpenAI-compatible provider
with the local base URL and the smart-free model name.

52
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Hermes configuration syntax can change, so verify its current
documentation. Other compatible SDKs use the same base URL and model.

53
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:35,000
Finally, suppose every currently verified-free candidate
is unavailable. ZeroSpend does not attempt a paid route.

54
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:46,000
It returns HTTP five hundred and three with FREE_CAPACITY_UNAVAILABLE,
zero paid attempts, and no immediate retry storm.

55
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:58,000
ZeroSpend cannot create free capacity, remove rate limits,
or guarantee that provider pricing will remain unchanged.

56
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:10,000
Verified free means the implemented evidence rule passed
at a recorded time. It is not a permanent promise.

57
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:22,000
Review provider terms, protect local credentials, and test model
quality against your own workload and capability requirements.

58
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:34,000
To explore the project without credentials, clone the
repository, enter the directory, and run npm run demo.

59
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:47,000
The source, setup guide, and deterministic demo are available
at github dot com slash sjfakharian slash zerospend.
